{"task": {"id": "I_kwDOUDROfM8AAAABVQLTRQ", "item_id": "PVTI_lAHOAHSLSM4BlR_zzg-wq0c", "project_id": "PVT_kwHOAHSLSM4BlR_z", "number": 43, "title": "Remove alternative ASR experiments and keep the hayamimi-style ReazonSpeech baseline", "body": "## Problem

The browser playground has accumulated multiple ASR comparison backends:

- Moonshine Voice;
- Moonshine + ReazonSpeech two-pass;
- ReazonSpeech offline;
- Japanese ReazonSpeech simulated streaming;
- ReazonSpeech ja-en simulated streaming;
- Whisper.

The comparison phase has been useful, but the active code now carries substantial experimental surface area that is no longer needed for the next product work.

Based on manual evaluation so far, the Japanese ReazonSpeech simulated-streaming path with hayamimi-inspired utterance handling is an acceptable current baseline for continuing development.

Keeping all previous experiments active increases code, model assets, tests, UI choices, and maintenance cost while the next work shifts toward reusable audio-input sources and broader product behavior.

## Expected outcome

Simplify the active repository so the browser speech-to-text implementation keeps only the current **Japanese ReazonSpeech simulated-streaming baseline** built around:

- sherpa-onnx / ReazonSpeech;
- Silero VAD;
- approximately 0.8 s pre-roll;
- approximately 0.5 s provisional recognition updates;
- approximately 0.35 s trailing-silence finalization;
- 12 s maximum utterance duration;
- the current worker separation / bounded inference behavior;
- the current evidence-based ReazonSpeech threading behavior.

Remove the other ASR experiments from the active product/playground code.

This is an intentional simplification, not a claim that ReazonSpeech is permanently selected as the final production ASR. Git history preserves the removed experiments and the architecture should remain replaceable.

Backward compatibility with the removed experimental modes is not required.

Completion criteria must be executable and verifiable by an AI agent.

## Scope

Remove the active implementation, UI, assets, preparation steps, tests, and current documentation that exist only to support:

- Moonshine Voice;
- Moonshine + ReazonSpeech two-pass;
- offline-only ReazonSpeech comparison mode;
- ReazonSpeech ja-en comparison mode;
- Whisper browser baseline.

Keep the Japanese hayamimi-style ReazonSpeech simulated-streaming path.

Simplify the UI accordingly:

- remove the ASR backend comparison selector if only one active backend remains;
- remove controls and diagnostics that only apply to removed backends;
- retain useful generic microphone, VAD, provisional/final transcript, timing, and error diagnostics.

Simplify implementation code rather than leaving dead abstractions for removed experiments.

Remove model/runtime assets and download/preparation logic that are no longer needed by the retained baseline.

Update tests so they cover the retained baseline without preserving compatibility tests for intentionally removed modes.

Update README and active testing/developer documentation to describe the retained ASR path as the **current baseline**, not as an irreversible production-backend decision.

Historical evidence documents may remain when they are useful records of completed experiments. Do not rewrite history merely to erase references to past work.

## Pre-merge acceptance criteria

- The browser UI exposes only the retained Japanese ReazonSpeech simulated-streaming ASR path.
- Moonshine, two-pass, offline ReazonSpeech comparison, ReazonSpeech ja-en comparison, and Whisper can no longer be selected or loaded by active application code.
- Code used exclusively by those removed modes is deleted.
- Runtime/model asset preparation no longer downloads or packages assets used exclusively by removed modes.
- Active tests no longer maintain compatibility for intentionally removed modes.
- Automated browser tests verify the retained baseline can load, start, produce provisional output, finalize an utterance, Stop, Cancel, and repeat without stale results.
- Existing tests for the retained Silero VAD, pre-roll, provisional scheduling, trailing-silence finalization, maximum utterance duration, worker isolation, bounded inference, and thread selection continue to pass where applicable.
- README/current documentation clearly states that this is the current development baseline and that removed experiments remain recoverable from Git history.
- No new ASR backend, audio-input architecture, diarization, meeting capture, or personalization feature is introduced in this Issue.
- The resulting implementation is materially smaller and does not retain speculative abstractions solely for removed experiments.

## Required post-merge verification

None.

## Context

The project intentionally started with disposable browser experiments to compare ASR approaches.

Relevant completed experiments include:

- #18 — initial browser ASR comparison;
- #25 / #29 — Moonshine Japanese improvements;
- #31 — Moonshine + ReazonSpeech two-pass;
- #33 / #35 — ReazonSpeech simulated streaming and hayamimi-style utterance handling;
- #37 / #38 — latency-oriented worker/thread improvements;
- #41 — ReazonSpeech ja-en simulated-streaming comparison;
- #2 — hayamimi design reference.

The current direction is to stop expanding ASR comparisons for now and use the hayamimi-style Japanese ReazonSpeech simulated-streaming implementation as the baseline while the project moves toward reusable audio-input sources and additional product use cases.

Removed implementations remain available through Git history if future evidence justifies revisiting them.
